<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Temple of Hera (Paestum)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 460 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Temple of Neptune</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paestum">Paestum</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> is a Doric temple built around 460 BCE, long known as the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neptune">Neptune</a>. Standing beside the older temple called the Basilica, it is among the best-preserved Greek temples anywhere, with almost all of its columns and entablature intact. It is one of the three standing temples that make Paestum a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-temple-of-hera-paestum.jpg" alt="Second Temple of Hera (Paestum)" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Norbert Nagel · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hera_temple_II_-_Paestum_-_Poseidonia_-_July_13th_2013_-_04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
